1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Sooner or later, you stop driving Cloud Code by hand and you start writing it into scripts,

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:06,000
pipelines, and a shared repo.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:13,000
This lesson covers how to run it non-interactively, how output streams back,

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:20,000
how the approval modes work, and where your Cloud.md and Settings.json live in this scenario.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Let's anchor three terms this lesson keeps coming back to.

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:30,000
First, headless mode. That's running Cloud Code non-interactively with the print flag.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,000
It takes your prompt, runs the task all the way to completion, prints the result to stand out, and exits.

8
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,000
No conversation required.

9
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:48,000
Second, settings.json. That's the JSON config file that holds your permissions, hooks, and environment variables.

10
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:53,000
And it layers across a user file, a project file, and a personal local one.

11
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Third, cloud.md. Those are markdown instructions loaded into context at the start of every session,

12
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,000
concatenated in scope order, with the project file read last.

13
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Headless settings, cloud.md, keep those three straight, and the rest of this lesson clicks into place.

14
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Here's the problem headless mode solves.

15
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Automation needs a command that finishes on its own, with nobody sitting at the keyboard.

16
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:24,000
The print flag, dash p, gives you exactly that.

17
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:29,000
You pass a prompt, Cloud runs the whole task, prints the result to stand out, and exits, just like a

18
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:30,000
normal command line tool.

19
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:38,000
Even better, it reads stand in, so you can pipe a file straight in and redirect the output somewhere else.

20
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Now here's the trap.

21
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:45,000
Leave the print flag off, and Cloud Code opens an interactive session and waits for you to type.

22
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Drop that into a CICD pipeline or a cron job, and the job just hangs forever, waiting on input that never comes.

23
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:59,000
Let's put the two modes side by side, because picking the wrong one is a common mistake.

24
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:02,000
Interactive mode is what you get by default.

25
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:08,000
You launch a session and work, turn by turn, approving actions as Cloud proposes them.

26
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:13,000
That's perfect when you're exploring, debugging, or building something new, and want a hand on the wheel.

27
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:16,000
Headless mode is the opposite posture.

28
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:21,000
You pass a single prompt, with the print flag, Cloud runs it straight through to completion, prints,

29
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,000
and exits, with no back and forth.

30
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:27,000
That's the mode you want for anything unattended.

31
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:33,000
A CICD step, a cron job, a shell script, that runs at 3 in the morning.

32
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:37,000
The rule of thumb if a human is watching, go interactive.

33
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:40,000
If a machine is running it, go headless.

34
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:44,000
When you run headless, you get to choose how the output comes back.

35
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:48,000
The default is plain text, one block printed when the run finishes.

36
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:55,000
Ask for JSON instead, and you get a structured object, the result text, plus the session ID, token

37
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:56,000
usage, and total cost,

38
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:00,000
which is ideal when a script needs to parse the outcome.

39
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:02,000
The third option is stream JSON.

40
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:10,000
Instead of waiting for the end, Cloud emits new line delimited JSON events, one per line, as the work happens.

41
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:16,000
That last one pairs with streaming input, which is the default and recommended path in the agent SDK.

42
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:22,000
So for a long running job, streaming lets your caller show progress live, instead of staring at a

43
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:25,000
frozen terminal, until the whole thing completes.

44
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:32,000
When Cloud runs unattended, the question is how much it does without asking first.

45
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:37,000
Cloud Code documents six permission modes, on a spectrum from full oversight to none.

46
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:39,000
Four are worth knowing.

47
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:47,000
Default, which the CLI surfaces as manual, pauses and asks before Cloud edits a file or runs a tool.

48
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:50,000
Maximum oversight, but it stops constantly.

49
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:52,000
No good for automation.

50
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:57,000
Accept edits applies file edits and the common file system commands without prompting.

51
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:01,000
Those shell commands beyond that trusted set still ask.

52
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:08,000
Plan mode proposes the actions first and waits for your go-ahead, so review comes before anything happens.

53
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:13,000
For unattended or CI runs, don't ask is the documented mode.

54
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:18,000
Only the tools you pre-approved run, and the session never waits on a prompt.

55
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:20,000
Match the mode to the risk.

56
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:24,000
The more the run does alone, the more your guardrails live somewhere else.

57
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:27,000
That last point deserves its own beat.

58
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:32,000
When you turn off the per-action approval prompt, you haven't removed the need for guardrails.

59
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:34,000
You've just moved where they live.

60
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:37,000
Three safeguards carry that weight.

61
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:40,000
First, permissions and settings dot JSON.

62
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Those decide which tools and commands Cloud is even allowed to run.

63
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:51,000
And unlike your instructions, they're enforced, not suggested. Second, hooks.

64
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:57,000
A pre-tool-use hook can inspect a pending action and block it before it fires, so a destructive

65
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:58,000
command never executes.

66
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:01,000
Third, plan mode.

67
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:06,000
Still the right call when you need a human to eyeball the plan before any action.

68
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:11,000
The takeaway, automation speed is only safe when those safeguards are wired in first.

69
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:15,000
Now, where do your standing instructions live?

70
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:17,000
Cloud.md is layered.

71
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:23,000
At the top, your user file at tilde slash dot cloud slash cloud dot md.

72
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:28,000
It applies to every project and loads first, the broadest, lowest-priority layer.

73
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:32,000
Next, the project file at the repo root.

74
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:36,000
You check it into version control so the team shares it, and it's read last.

75
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:38,000
But nothing enforces precedence.

76
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:45,000
The layers are simply concatenated into context, and if two rules conflict, Cloud may pick either one,

77
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:49,000
so keep the layers consistent rather than counting on the project file to win.

78
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:54,000
A cloud dot md deeper in the tree scopes rules to one folder.

79
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:57,000
Loading when Cloud works with files there, here's the trap.

80
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:01,000
Every one of these files counts against your context window.

81
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:06,000
It's real tokens, not free memory, so keep them lean and specific.

82
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:12,000
A couple of tools get a fresh repo configured without starting from a blank page.

83
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:17,000
The first is repo init, run slash init, and Cloud scans the code base,

84
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:23,000
picks up your structure, conventions and build commands, and drafts a starter cloud dot md for you.

85
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:26,000
Treat that as a first pass, though, not a finished product.

86
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:32,000
You read it, correct it, and trim it, because it's a draft to refine, not a config to trust blindly.

87
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:35,000
The second is settings dot json.

88
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:40,000
That's where your permissions, hooks, and environment variables live, checked into the project,

89
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:42,000
so the team shares one setup.

90
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:48,000
And when you have personal tweaks you don't want committed, those go in settings dot local dot json,

91
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:51,000
which stays out of version control.

92
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:55,000
Instructions in cloud dot md, enforcement in settings dot json.

93
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:58,000
Two different layers kept separate.

94
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:01,000
Let's frame the big picture.

95
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:05,000
Headless mode with the print flag is how cloud code runs unattended,

96
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:09,000
and stream json is how you watch a long job progress.

97
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:14,000
When you drop the approval prompt, your guardrails move into permissions and hooks.

98
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:21,000
Layer cloud dot md by scope, draft it with init, and remember every line costs context.

99
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:24,000
That wraps up this lesson. I'll catch you in the next one.

